    {
      "slug": "return-policy-backfire",
      "script": "My manager fired a cashier for approving one lenient return, then posted a memo: every return exactly per written policy, no exceptions, no discretion, ever. Fine by me. I laminated the policy and followed it to the letter. Torn tag but missing receipt? Denied, per section four. Opened box, no receipt, forty seconds past register close? Denied. A regular with six years of loyalty got the same cold no as a stranger. Complaints piled up, corporate started calling, and the manager got summoned upstairs twice in one week. Three weeks later, he tried returning his own broken espresso machine without a receipt. I smiled, pulled out the laminated sheet, and read section four out loud. He stood speechless while the whole line behind him started clapping. His exceptions returned before his refund did.",
      "editor_note": "Tightened the setup, preserved every protected number and shot phrase, and replaced the applause-only ending with a dry callback."
    },
    {
      "slug": "kids-eat-free-fine-print",
      "script": "A mom at my restaurant tried to use our kids eat free promo for her seventeen-year-old and four random neighbor kids she called basically family. I explained the promo covers one child per paying adult, ages twelve and under. She demanded whoever's actually in charge and announced she would never come back. I pointed at the fine print taped right on the menu, which she had been reading upside down while yelling. Turned out she was recording the whole thing for a complaint video, with the camera pointed the wrong way. It captured only the ceiling and her own rant. She posted it anyway. The comments read the menu for her within minutes. By morning, three hundred people were tagging our page just to say thank you. The camera missed us. The fine print did not.",
      "editor_note": "Cut filler, kept every protected number and shot phrase, and landed the story on a short fine-print callback."
    }
  ],
